Phase 93 (Dynamic C Runtime) — export musl's `__init_tp` so the m3OS foreign
loader can finish the thread-control-block setup.

In a normal dynamic musl process the dynamic linker (`__dls3`) calls
`__init_tp(__copy_tls(mem))` to install the thread pointer AND populate the
musl-internal `struct pthread` fields that the rest of libc reads:
`self`, `locale` (= `&libc.global_locale`), `tid`, `robust_list.head`,
`detach_state`, `sysinfo`, and `next`/`prev`. m3OS's from-scratch Rust loader
replaces `__dls3` and lays out the TLS block + DTV itself (it can compute the
main-exe base from `AT_PHDR`, which musl's own `static_init_tls` mis-derives for
a dynamic program), but it cannot set `locale`/`robust_list`/etc. correctly
because those reference libc-internal globals. A minimal TCB (just `self`+`dtv`)
runs trivial programs (e.g. a printf/malloc hello) but a real interpreter like
CPython dereferences `self->locale` during startup and faults on the NULL.

`__init_tp` already does exactly the right field setup — the only blocker is
that it is declared `hidden`, so the foreign loader cannot resolve it via the
dynamic symbol table. Drop the `hidden` qualifier on its declaration so it is
exported; the loader then `__init_tp(tcb)`s after building the TLS block, and
every libc-internal pthread field is populated by musl's own code.

--- a/src/internal/pthread_impl.h
+++ b/src/internal/pthread_impl.h
@@ -141,4 +141,4 @@
 void *__tls_get_addr(tls_mod_off_t *);
-hidden int __init_tp(void *);
+int __init_tp(void *);
 hidden void *__copy_tls(unsigned char *);
 hidden void __reset_tls();
